[Circular Import Error] (noted by Claude): 2026-09-04 12:00

Incident: Circular import between reporting.py and summarize.py

Context:
After extracting cli_helpers.py, file_io.py, and trial_loading.py, file imports were reorganized.
- reporting.py imported TrialKey, QualityContext from summarize.py
- summarize.py imported print_analysis_report from reporting.py
- Result: ImportError on module load

Error message:
```
Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "...\summarize.py", line 25, in <module>
    from reporting import print_analysis_report
  File "...\reporting.py", line 9, in <module>
    from summarize import TrialKey, QualityContext, calculate_statistics
ImportError: cannot import name 'print_analysis_report'
```

Root cause:
Shared dataclasses (TrialKey, QualityContext, Trial, TrialSet, AggregationState, etc.) were defined in summarize.py but needed by reporting.py and trial_loading.py, creating bidirectional dependencies.

Resolution:
Created data_models.py module containing all shared dataclasses with no logic dependencies. Updated imports:
- reporting.py: imports TrialKey, QualityContext from data_models.py
- trial_loading.py: imports Trial, QualityContext, AggregationState from data_models.py
- summarize.py: imports all data models from data_models.py
- No circular references; all modules compile cleanly

Impact: Resolved without code loss; actually improved architecture by isolating data models.

Lesson: Shared data structures should be in separate module to avoid circular dependencies; separating data from logic prevents import cycles.

